  <h2>Texture import contract</h2>
  <p>A weapon texture used in 3D <strong>MUST</strong> be imported as <strong>VRAM-compressed with mipmaps</strong> (<code>compress/mode=2</code>, <code>mipmaps/generate=true</code>) — the state Godot's editor produces automatically via <code>detect_3d</code> the first time a texture is used in a 3D scene. Every working basic model satisfies this (it ships <code>.s3tc</code>/<code>.etc2</code> variants).</p>
  <p><strong>Known violation (the ein set).</strong> The ein textures were imported on a headless build box, so <code>detect_3d</code> never ran — they are stuck as plain 2D lossless (<code>compress/mode=0</code>, no mipmaps, <code>detect_3d/compress_to=1</code> still pending). They render correctly on the Forward+ path and on the macOS GL-over-Metal Compatibility path, but report as <strong>untextured on Windows/NVIDIA under the shipped <code>gl_compatibility</code> renderer</strong>. The fix is to normalize the ein texture imports to match the contract (VRAM + mipmaps) and republish the pack. Tracked against this page.</p>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Binding (which surfaces carry an albedo texture) is checked by loading each distinct model headlessly — renderer-agnostic, so it confirms data integrity but <em>not</em> on-GPU rendering.</li>
    <li>Actual rendering is renderer- and driver-specific: the <code>gl_compatibility</code> backend on Windows/NVIDIA is the reference target (it is what ships). A Mac repro is necessary-but-not-sufficient — a texture that renders on Apple GL can still fail on native GL.</li>
  </ul>
</Concept>
